// What the screens say about each weekly portion of Leviticus (Vayikra). The layout of each entry is
// explained at the top of lessons-genesis.js. Verse numbers follow the Hebrew Bible (as Sefaria does);
// in chapters 5 and 6 they differ from English Bibles.
window.TORAH_LESSONS = window.TORAH_LESSONS || {};
Object.assign(window.TORAH_LESSONS, {

  'Vayikra': {
    meaning: 'And He called',
    summary: 'From the newly built Mishkan, God calls to Moses and teaches the laws of the <i>korbanot</i>, the offerings: the burnt offering given entirely to God, the grain offering, the peace offering shared in a meal, and the offerings that bring atonement for wrongs. Each can be brought as a bull, a sheep or goat, a pair of birds, or even a handful of flour, depending on what a person can afford.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Coming close', text: 'The Hebrew word for an offering, <i>korban</i>, comes from <i>karov</i>, “close.” An offering was a way of coming closer to God. After the Temple, the prophet Hosea’s words became the model: our prayers take the place of the offerings.', source: 'Leviticus 1:2 · Hosea 14:3' },
      { title: 'Every gift counts', text: 'A rich person brings a bull, a poorer one birds, the poorest a handful of flour, and each is “a pleasing aroma to the LORD.” The Mishnah: giving much or giving little is the same, as long as the heart is turned toward Heaven.', source: 'Leviticus 1–2 · Mishnah Menachot 13:11' },
      { title: 'Mistakes can be fixed', text: 'Much of this portion is about offerings for wrongs done by accident. The Torah expects people, even priests and leaders, to make mistakes, and gives them a way to own them and move on.', source: 'Leviticus 4' },
      { title: 'Happy are the leaders who admit it', text: 'For a ruler who sins, the Torah says <i>asher</i>, “when,” not “if.” Rashi reads it as <i>ashrei</i>, “happy”: happy is the generation whose leader admits his mistakes.', source: 'Leviticus 4:22 · Rashi' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Leviticus 1:9', headline: 'The Ramban: the animal on the altar is standing in for you.', text: 'The burnt offering is cut apart, its blood dashed, every piece burned: “a fire-offering, a pleasing aroma to the LORD.” Maimonides explained sacrifice as a cure for idolatry: slaughter for God the very animals Egypt worshipped. The Ramban calls that a light bandage on a deep wound, as if God’s table were a cure for fools. He prefers this: you sinned in thought, word and deed, so you lay on your hands, confess aloud, and watch the organs of thought and desire burn, knowing your own blood should have been spilled but for God’s kindness in taking a substitute.', try: 'Name one wrong you got away with this year. Today, do one thing that costs you something to set it right.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 1:17', headline: 'God wanted the poor man’s bird burned feathers and all.', text: 'A person who can’t afford a bull or a sheep brings a turtledove. The priest burns it whole, “with its feathers.” Rashi is frank: is there anyone who can smell burning feathers without being disgusted? Then why? So that the altar will look full and adorned with the poor man’s offering, since a bird with its feathers makes a bigger show. And the verse ends with the same words used for the bull: “a pleasing aroma to the LORD.” Rashi: whether one gives much or little, it is the same, so long as the heart is turned to heaven.', try: 'When someone gives you something small today, a favor, a gift, a bit of help, thank them in a way that makes it look as big as it felt to them.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 2:13', headline: 'Every offering carried salt from the waters that wept.', text: '“Do not leave out the salt of your God’s covenant; with all your offerings bring salt.” Why salt? Rashi says a covenant was made in the six days of creation: when God divided the waters above from the waters below, the lower waters were promised a place on the altar, as salt and as the water poured out on Sukkot. Rabbeinu Bachya brings the midrash behind it: the lower waters cried, “Woe to us, that we did not merit to be close to our Creator.” So nothing goes up to God without the part of creation that felt left below.', try: 'Think of someone who feels left out of the part of something that matters, at work, in the family, among friends. Give them a real role in it today.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 3:16', headline: 'Maimonides: “all the fat is God’s” means feed the hungry your best.', text: 'The peace offering is the one the family gets to eat. But first the richest parts, the fat around the kidneys and the liver, go up in smoke: “all the fat is the LORD’s.” In the ancient world the fat was the delicacy. Maimonides turns the verse into a rule for life: anything given for God’s sake should be the finest you have. If you build a house of prayer, make it nicer than your own home. If you feed someone hungry, feed them the best and sweetest food on your table. If you clothe someone, give your good clothes.', try: 'The next thing you give away today, food, clothes, an hour, give the good one and keep the spare for yourself.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 4:22', headline: 'The Torah says “if” a priest sins, but “when” a ruler does.', text: 'The laws of the sin offering climb a ladder: “if the anointed priest sins,” “if the whole community errs,” “if one person sins.” Only for the ruler, the <i>nasi</i>, does the wording change: <i>asher nasi yecheta</i>, “when a ruler sins.” Leaders will get things wrong; the only question is what they do next. Rashi hears <i>ashrei</i>, “happy,” in that word: happy is the generation whose leader brings an offering even for a mistake he didn’t mean, for how much more will he repent of what he did on purpose.', try: 'If you lead anything, a team, a project, a household, name one mistake of yours today to the people it affected, before anyone else brings it up.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 5:1', headline: 'Swearing “I saw nothing” is a deliberate sin the altar still covers.', text: '“If a person hears a call to testify, and he saw or knew, and does not tell, he bears his guilt.” Sin offerings are usually only for mistakes; a deliberate sin is beyond the altar. This one is different. The Mishnah (Shevuot 4:2) rules that witnesses who swear “we know nothing” bring an offering even if they lied on purpose, as long as they knew what they had seen. The Torah expects that people will keep quiet knowingly, out of fear or loyalty, and calls it a sin that still has a way back.', try: 'Is there something you saw, at work or among friends, that someone needs you to say out loud? Say it today, to the person who needs to hear it.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 5:21', headline: 'Cheat someone with no witnesses and you have denied God.', text: 'The Torah calls it “a betrayal of the LORD” when someone lies to a neighbor about a deposit or a loan. Why God, when only the neighbor lost? Rabbi Akiva explains, in Rashi: loans and sales are made before witnesses or with a contract, but a person who leaves a deposit with a friend wants no one to know except the Third who is between them. Deny the deposit and you have denied Him. The order of repair is fixed too: the Mishnah rules that a guilt offering brought before the money is returned does not count.', try: 'Settle one money matter today that only you and one other person know about: a debt, a borrowed thing, an unpaid share. Pay it before you pray about anything else.' },
    ],
  },

  'Tzav': {
    meaning: 'Command',
    summary: 'God gives the priests their instructions: the fire on the altar must never go out; how to handle each kind of offering; and which parts belong to the priests. Then Moses installs Aaron and his sons as priests in a seven-day ceremony: washing them, dressing them in their priestly clothes, anointing them with oil, and bringing the offerings, while they stay at the entrance of the Tent day and night.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Urgency where it costs', text: 'The portion opens “Command Aaron.” Rashi says “command” means urging, because extra encouragement is needed where a mitzvah costs money: the burnt offering went entirely on the altar, with nothing left over for anyone.', source: 'Leviticus 6:2 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Take out the ashes', text: 'Each morning the priest’s first job is to remove yesterday’s ashes from the altar, dressed in plain linen. Even the holiest person starts the day by cleaning up. No job is beneath you.', source: 'Leviticus 6:3–4' },
      { title: 'Thanks deserves a feast', text: 'The thanksgiving offering came with forty loaves of bread, all to be eaten within a day and a night. That much bread in that little time means you have to invite people. Gratitude is meant to be shared.', source: 'Leviticus 7:12–15 · Menachot 77b' },
      { title: 'Do it all the way', text: 'The portion ends: “Aaron and his sons did all the things the LORD had commanded.” Rashi says this is praise: they didn’t turn right or left. Doing exactly what you said you’d do is a quiet kind of greatness.', source: 'Leviticus 8:36 · Rashi' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Leviticus 6:3', headline: 'The priests fought over who got to take out the ashes.', text: 'Each morning a priest in his linen vestments shovels up yesterday’s ashes from the altar: the first task of the day. The Mishnah (Yoma 2:1–2) says the priests wanted it so badly that they raced up the ramp for it, until one shoved another and broke his leg, and the court switched to a lottery. The Talmud (Yoma 23a) records worse: a priest who lost the race stabbed the winner. The boy’s father, finding him still alive, worried that the knife would become impure. The verdict: the purity of vessels mattered more to them than blood.', try: 'Think of something good you care about, a cause, a practice, a project, that has made you hard on the people around it. Today, put one of those people ahead of it.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 6:18', headline: 'The silent Amidah began as a way to hide other people’s sins.', text: '“In the place where the burnt offering is slaughtered, the sin offering shall be slaughtered.” A burnt offering can be a free gift; a sin offering means you did something wrong. Same spot, so no one watching in the courtyard could tell which one you came with. Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai draws a line from here to daily prayer (Sotah 32b): the sages had us say the Amidah in a whisper so as not to embarrass sinners. Every time a room falls silent to pray, it is sheltering confessions no one else will ever hear.', try: 'Someone told you, or you found out, about a mistake they made. Keep it completely to yourself today, even from the people you tell everything.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 7:12', headline: 'The thanksgiving offering is too much bread to eat alone.', text: 'Who brings a thanksgiving offering? Rashi lists the four of Psalm 107: someone who crossed the sea, crossed the desert, got out of prison, or recovered from illness. With the animal come forty loaves, and all of it must be eaten in one day and a night, less time than an ordinary peace offering. Why so much food and so little time? The Netziv answers: so that you will have to invite a crowd and tell them the story of what God did for you. And the midrash says that when every other offering ends, this one will remain.', try: 'Think of a time you were rescued: a scan that came back clean, a crisis that passed, someone who got you out. Tell the story tonight to people who weren’t there.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 8:7', headline: 'Moses ran the priesthood for a week, thinking it was his.', text: 'Moses washes Aaron, dresses him in the tunic, the sash, the robe, the breastplate, and pours the anointing oil on his head. He is dressing his older brother for a role that, the midrash says, Moses believed was his own. Rabbi Chelbo teaches (Vayikra Rabbah 11:6) that for all seven days of the installation Moses served as High Priest, and on the seventh God told him: not yours, your brother’s. The midrash calls it the price of the burning bush, where Moses begged, “Send someone else.” Moses does all of it anyway, exactly “as the LORD commanded.”', try: 'Is there someone who got the role, the credit or the chance you wanted? Do one thing today that helps them succeed in it.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 8:15', headline: 'The new altar needed atonement for gifts given under pressure.', text: 'Moses puts the blood of the sin offering on the altar’s horns “and purified the altar.” A brand-new altar: what sin could it carry? The Ramban quotes the sages’ answer from the Torat Kohanim. When the call went out to give to the Mishkan, people pressured each other, and some gave without their full will. So the altar itself needed atonement, for God does not want what was taken by pressure. The most generous campaign in the Torah, stopped because there was too much, still had people in it who gave because they felt watched.', try: 'Is there a gift, a donation or a favor you are pushing someone toward? Ask for it today in a way that makes no as easy to say as yes.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 8:23', headline: 'The High Priest and the healed outcast are marked the same way.', text: 'Moses dabs the ram’s blood on Aaron’s right earlobe, right thumb and right big toe. Apart from the priests, only one person in the Torah is ever marked like this: the person healed of <i>tzara’at</i>, on the eighth day after his return to the camp (14:14). Ibn Ezra says the blood atones for Aaron’s life, a life for a life, and compares it to the night Zipporah circumcised her son and cast the blood at Moses’ feet. The holiest office in Israel begins with the same rite as coming home from exile: a life that needed ransoming.', try: 'Think of someone who has just come back from something hard: illness, divorce, rehab, a layoff. Treat them today the way you would treat someone just promoted.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 8:34', headline: 'The High Priest could not atone for Israel without a wife.', text: 'For seven days Aaron and his sons may not leave the Tent’s entrance. Rashi brings the sages’ reading: from here the High Priest is taken from his home for seven days before every Yom Kippur. The Mishnah (Yoma 1:1) adds a startling detail. A backup priest was readied in case he became unfit, and Rabbi Yehudah says they even readied a backup wife, in case his wife died. The Torah says he atones “for himself and his household,” and his household is his wife. The man set apart from everyone still cannot do the holiest work alone.', try: 'Tell the person closest to you one specific way they make your work, or your life’s work, possible. Say it today, in words.' },
    ],
  },

  'Shmini': {
    meaning: 'Eighth',
    summary: 'On the eighth day, Aaron and his sons begin serving as priests, and fire comes from God to consume the offering as the people shout and fall on their faces. Then Aaron’s sons Nadav and Avihu bring a “strange fire” that God hadn’t commanded, and fire consumes them. Aaron is silent. The portion ends with the laws of kosher food: which animals, fish, birds and insects may be eaten.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Silence in grief', text: 'When his two sons die, “Aaron was silent.” Sometimes there are no words, and that’s all right. The Talmud says the merit of visiting mourners is in the silence: just being there.', source: 'Leviticus 10:3 · Berakhot 6b' },
      { title: 'Good intentions aren’t enough', text: 'Nadav and Avihu offered a fire “that He had not commanded them.” Enthusiasm without listening can do harm. Passion needs boundaries.', source: 'Leviticus 10:1–2' },
      { title: 'Know the difference', text: 'The priests are told “to tell apart the holy and the ordinary, the impure and the pure,” and to teach it. Much of life is learning to tell what matters from what doesn’t.', source: 'Leviticus 10:10–11' },
      { title: 'Holiness on your plate', text: 'The food laws end with “You shall be holy, for I am holy.” Even eating, the most everyday thing we do, can be done with care and on purpose.', source: 'Leviticus 11:44–45' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Leviticus 9:7', headline: 'The first offering asked of the calf’s maker is a calf.', text: 'On the eighth day Aaron’s first offering is his own sin offering: a calf. Rashi says the choice is deliberate: through this calf God forgives him for the golden one. Aaron hangs back; Moses has to tell him, “Come near to the altar.” The Sifra gives two reasons: he was ashamed, or he looked at the horned altar, saw an ox, and was afraid. Moses tells him: why were you chosen, if not for this? The Torah gives its holiest job to the man with the most reason to feel unfit, and makes him look straight at what he did.', try: 'Is there a responsibility you’ve been avoiding because of an old failure in that same area? Take one real step back into it today.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 9:23', headline: 'Aaron did everything right, and still heaven stayed silent.', text: 'Aaron brings every offering exactly as commanded, lifts his hands and blesses the people, and nothing happens. No fire. Rashi, quoting the Sifra, gives his anguish words: “God is angry with me; it is because of me that the Presence has not come down.” He turns to his brother: “Moses, is this what you do to me? I went in at your word, and I have been shamed.” So Moses goes into the Tent with him, they pray together, and only then does the fire come. Doing it right was not enough; the two of them had to ask.', try: 'If something you did by the book still isn’t working, stop trying harder alone. Today, ask one person to sit down with you and face it together.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 10:3', headline: 'Aaron said nothing, and for that God spoke to him alone.', text: 'Fire comes out and consumes Nadav and Avihu on the greatest day of Aaron’s life. Why? Rabbi Eliezer says they ruled on law in front of Moses, their teacher; Rabbi Yishmael, that they came in drunk. Moses offers his brother a theology, “Through those near to Me I will be made holy,” and, Rashi says, adds that the boys were greater than either of them. Aaron answers none of it: <i>vayidom Aharon</i>, “Aaron was silent.” The Talmud (Zevachim 115b) says the silence was rewarded; Rashi says how: God’s next words were addressed to Aaron alone.', try: 'When someone near you is grieving or hurting this week, don’t explain or fix anything. Sit with them and let your silence do the work.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 10:12', headline: 'The word “remaining” means Aaron’s other sons had been condemned too.', text: 'Hours after the fire, Moses speaks to Aaron “and to Elazar and Itamar, his remaining sons,” and tells them to eat the offering. Rashi hears a darker meaning in “remaining”: remaining from death. All of Aaron’s sons, he says, had been sentenced over the golden calf, since Deuteronomy says God was angry enough with Aaron “to destroy him,” and destruction means losing children. Moses prayed for his brother then, and the prayer cancelled half the decree. So the same day holds both: two sons lost to fire, two alive because of a prayer said for Aaron long before.', try: 'Someone may have prayed for you, defended you or covered for you without your knowing. Today, do that for someone else, quietly, and don’t tell them.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 10:20', headline: 'Moses’ anger made him wrong, and his honesty made him great.', text: 'Moses finds that a sin offering was burned instead of eaten, and he is furious. Out of respect for his grieving brother, Rashi says, he aims his anger at Aaron’s sons instead. Aaron answers for them: after what happened today, should I eat holy food? The Sifrei counts this among the three times Moses fell into anger and so into error. Then the verse: “Moses heard, and it was good in his eyes.” The Talmud (Zevachim 101b) says he wasn’t ashamed: he didn’t say “I was never told,” but “I heard the law, and I forgot it.”', try: 'Think of the last argument where you lost your temper. Go back to that person today and admit the specific thing you got wrong, with no excuses.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 11:19', headline: 'The bird named for kindness is not kosher.', text: 'Among the forbidden birds is the <i>chasidah</i>, the stork. Why the name? The Talmud (Chullin 63a), quoted by Rashi, says it does <i>chasidut</i>, kindness, “with its companions,” sharing its food with them. So why would a bird named for kindness be unclean? A well-known Hasidic teaching answers from those very words: because it is kind only to its companions. Generosity that never leaves your circle, your family, your friends, your kind, can look like virtue and still be a form of selfishness.', try: 'Do one generous thing today for someone entirely outside your circle: not family, not a friend, not someone like you.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 11:45', headline: 'The Exodus would have been worth it for the food laws alone.', text: 'Elsewhere God says “I brought you out of Egypt.” Here, closing the food laws, the verb changes: “I am the LORD who brought you up.” The Talmud (Bava Metzia 61b) quotes the school of Rabbi Yishmael: had I brought Israel out of Egypt only so that they would not defile themselves with creeping things, it would have been enough, and it is an elevation for them. Ten plagues and a split sea, for something as small as what you will not put in your mouth. Freedom is proven in daily, unglamorous choices, not just the great escape.', try: 'Pick one small daily habit, what you eat, how you speak, when you put the phone down, and keep it today as if your freedom depended on it.' },
    ],
  },

  'Tazria': {
    meaning: 'She conceives',
    summary: 'After giving birth, a mother brings offerings when she returns to the sanctuary. Then the Torah turns to <i>tzara’at</i>, a skin disease (often translated “leprosy,” though it isn’t) that the priest examines. A person found to have it lives outside the camp until it heals. The same kind of mark can also appear on clothing.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Words can wound', text: 'The rabbis linked tzara’at to <i>lashon hara</i>, harmful talk; Miriam got it after speaking against Moses. The Talmud reads <i>metzora</i>, a person with tzara’at, as <i>motzi ra</i>: “one who spreads bad things.”', source: 'Numbers 12 · Arakhin 15b' },
      { title: 'Loneliness teaches', text: 'The person with tzara’at lives alone outside the camp for a while. Rashi says: their words separated people, so they’re separated too. Feeling what loneliness is like teaches you not to cause it.', source: 'Leviticus 13:46 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Look twice', text: 'The priest examines carefully, often waiting seven days and looking again before deciding. Before you call something bad, take a second look.', source: 'Leviticus 13:4–6' },
      { title: 'Birth is holy', text: 'The portion begins with a new mother. The Torah treats giving birth as an awesome, life-changing event that deserves its own time and its own offerings.', source: 'Leviticus 12' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Leviticus 12:6', headline: 'Rabbi Shimon: a new mother atones for a vow made in agony.', text: 'After giving birth, a mother brings a burnt offering and a sin offering. A sin offering, for having a child? The students of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai asked him that (Niddah 31b). His answer: when a woman crouches to give birth, the pain is so great that she swears she will never be with her husband again. Then she breaks that oath, and the offering is for that. Rav Yosef pokes holes in the explanation, and the Talmud records his objections. But the image stays: a law that takes a woman’s pain seriously enough to imagine what she cried out.', try: 'Think of something you said in pain, to someone or about yourself, that you didn’t really mean. Take it back today, out loud, to whoever heard it.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 13:14', headline: 'The Torah delays a diagnosis so a groom can finish his wedding.', text: '“On the day raw flesh appears in him, he is impure.” Why “on the day”? Rashi says the word teaches that there are days when the priest examines and days when he doesn’t. A bridegroom is given all seven days of his wedding celebration, for himself, his clothes and his house; on a festival, everyone is given the whole festival. The mark is real, and it will still be looked at. But the Torah would rather delay bad news than spoil a wedding or a holiday. Telling the truth well includes choosing when to tell it.', try: 'If you have hard news or criticism to give someone, ask whether today is the day for it. If it isn’t, choose the day and put it in your calendar.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 13:23', headline: 'A scar that stays in its place is not a disease.', text: 'A boil heals, and a white spot appears where it was. The priest waits seven days. If the spot stays where it is and doesn’t spread, “it is the scar of the boil, and the priest declares him pure.” Rashi, following the Targum, says it is only the mark the inflammation left in the flesh. Ibn Ezra says what the priest watches for: real afflictions move from place to place. The Torah draws a line we often forget: an old wound that still shows is not the same as one that is still spreading. A scar is history, not sickness.', try: 'Name one old hurt you still carry. Ask honestly whether it is still spreading into how you treat people now. If it is, tell someone you trust about it today.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 13:25', headline: 'The Mishnah bans judging a mark in the wrong light.', text: 'A burn has healed and a white patch appears in its scar. “The priest shall look at it,” and everything depends on what he sees. The Mishnah (Negaim 2:2) is strict about how he looks: not at dawn or dusk, not indoors, not on a cloudy day, because then a dull spot looks bright; and not at noon, because then a bright spot looks dull. Only in the middle hours of the morning or afternoon. The sages knew that what we see depends on the light we see it in, and a verdict in the wrong light can ruin someone.', try: 'Before you judge someone today, ask what light you’re seeing them in: your tiredness, your mood, a story you heard secondhand. Wait for a better hour before deciding.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 13:30', headline: 'A priest may judge anyone’s marks except his own.', text: 'A patch appears in the hair of the head or the beard. “The priest shall look at the mark,” and if it is deeper than the skin, he declares it impure. The Mishnah (Negaim 2:5) adds who may not look: a person may examine every mark except his own, and Rabbi Meir says, not his relatives’ either. It also rules that no sage may release his own vows. The rabbis did not trust even the expert’s eye, his learning or his piety, when the skin was his own or his family’s. The closer the case, the less you can see.', try: 'Ask someone who loves you but tells the truth to name one thing about you, or your family, that you can’t see. Listen without defending yourself.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 13:46', headline: 'The slanderer sits alone because he made others alone.', text: 'The person with <i>tzara’at</i> tears his clothes, covers his lip, calls out “Impure, impure!” and “shall dwell alone, outside the camp.” Other impure people may stay together; why is he alone? Rashi gives the sages’ answer (Arachin 16b): his slander parted husband from wife and friend from friend, so he is parted from everyone. But the same verses carry mercy. The Talmud (Sotah 32b) reads his cry not as humiliation but as a request: he announces his pain so that many will pray for him. The isolation is the consequence; the crying out is the way back.', try: 'Think of two people your words have pushed apart, even a little. Today, say something to one of them that brings them closer to the other.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 13:55', headline: 'Maimonides: the stained garment was the last warning before the skin.', text: 'A marked garment is washed and shut away. If the mark hasn’t changed, the cloth is burned. Maimonides insists this is no natural mold but a sign, sent to warn against <i>lashon hara</i>, evil speech. It comes in stages. First the walls of the speaker’s house change color; if he repents, the house is pure. If he goes on, the leather he sits on changes; then the clothes he wears; and only then his own skin. At every stage he can stop. The garment burning in front of him is the last warning before it reaches him.', try: 'Notice the next time today you start to say something about a person who isn’t there. Stop mid-sentence, and say something true and good about them instead.' },
    ],
  },

  'Metzora': {
    meaning: 'The person with tzara’at',
    summary: 'When a person with tzara’at heals, the priest goes outside the camp to see them and performs a ceremony with two birds, cedar wood, crimson yarn and hyssop. After seven days and more offerings, they return to the community. The same kind of mark can appear on the walls of a house, which may need to be scraped or even torn down. The portion ends with laws of purity about the body.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Go out to them', text: 'The priest must go “outside the camp” to the person who was sent away. Healing starts when someone comes out to meet you where you are.', source: 'Leviticus 14:3' },
      { title: 'From cedar to hyssop', text: 'The ceremony uses a tall cedar and a low hyssop plant. Rashi says tzara’at came from arrogance, and the cure is to lower yourself, like the hyssop.', source: 'Leviticus 14:4 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Chattering birds', text: 'Why birds? Rashi says tzara’at comes from chatter, and birds chatter all day long. The cure for careless words starts with noticing how much we talk.', source: 'Leviticus 14:4 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Hidden treasure', text: 'Rashi says that when a house had to be torn open, the Israelites sometimes found treasure the old owners had hidden in the walls. Something that looks like a disaster can uncover something precious.', source: 'Leviticus 14:34 · Rashi' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Leviticus 14:4', headline: 'The healing kit holds a cedar, a worm’s dye and a weed.', text: 'On the day of healing, the priest goes out of the camp to the person with <i>tzara’at</i>, carrying two birds, a stick of cedar, crimson wool and hyssop. Rashi decodes each one. Birds, because <i>tzara’at</i> comes for slander, and birds chatter all day long. Cedar, the tallest of trees, because it also comes for arrogance. And the cure? The crimson dye comes from a worm, <i>tola’at</i>, and hyssop is a low shrub: let him lower himself like the worm and the hyssop. The ritual tells him how he got here, and the way back, in things he can hold.', try: 'Find one place this week where you were the cedar: the one who knew best, talked over others, needed to win. Go back today and take the lower seat.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 14:14', headline: 'Whispered slander needs a guilt offering, as if robbing the Temple.', text: 'To come home, the healed person brings an <i>asham</i>, a guilt offering, and its blood goes on his right ear, thumb and toe. Why a guilt offering, the offering for misusing what belongs to the sanctuary? The Sforno explains: the sins that bring <i>tzara’at</i>, slander and arrogance, trespass against God’s own holiness. Slander is usually done in secret, so the victim can’t answer, and Isaiah describes such people saying, “Who sees us?” The Sforno reads the prophet as saying the secrecy is worse than the words, because it assumes God isn’t watching.', try: 'Before you say anything today about someone who isn’t in the room, ask: would I say it if they walked in right now? If not, don’t.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 14:21', headline: 'The poor may give like the rich, but never the reverse.', text: '“If he is poor and cannot afford it,” the healed person brings one lamb and two birds instead of three lambs, and the Torah keeps repeating, “whatever he can afford.” The Mishnah (Negaim 14:12) adds a rule that cuts only one way. A poor person who stretches and brings the rich offering has fulfilled his duty. A rich person who brings the poor offering has not. You may always give beyond your means, never below them. In the Torah, the sliding scale is protection for the poor, and it is never a loophole for anyone who could do more.', try: 'Look at one thing you give, to charity, to family, to a friend in need, and ask whether it matches your real means now. If it doesn’t, raise it today.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 14:36', headline: 'Say “I don’t have one,” and the house plague exposes everything.', text: 'When a mark appears on a house’s walls, the priest orders it emptied before he looks, so that its contents won’t become impure. Rashi notes how carefully the Torah protects the owner’s property, down to his cheap clay pots. But the Talmud (Yoma 11b) sees why the plague came: the owner refused to lend his things, and told his neighbors, “I don’t have one.” Now everything he owns is carried out into the street for all to see. In one act, the Torah protects his pots and exposes his lie.', try: 'Is there something you own that a neighbor, friend or relative could use, and you’ve kept to yourself? Offer it to them today, before they ask.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 15:13', headline: 'Maimonides: the mikveh changes nothing in the body, only in the will.', text: 'When a man’s discharge stops, he counts seven clean days and “bathes his flesh in living water, and he is pure.” Maimonides is blunt about what this is not: impurity is not mud or filth that water can wash away. Purity is a decree, and someone who immerses without intending to become pure has not immersed at all. Then he finds the hint inside it: you go down, nothing in your body changes, and you come up pure because you resolved to. So too one who decides to leave bad habits and immerses his soul in “the waters of knowledge.”', try: 'Pick one habit you want to leave behind. Today, mark the decision with a physical act, a shower, a walk, a cleared desk, and make the decision while you do it.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 15:19', headline: 'Rabbi Meir: the separation is so she’ll be loved like a bride.', text: '“When a woman has her flow… she shall be in her separation seven days.” The laws of <i>niddah</i> can read like a list of what is forbidden. Rabbi Meir, in the Talmud (Niddah 31b), reads them as a teaching about love. If a husband had his wife always, he would grow too used to her, even tired of her. So the Torah set days apart, so that when she becomes pure again she will be as dear to him as on the day she stood under the wedding canopy. Desire, he understood, needs distance as well as closeness.', try: 'Think of someone close to you whom you’ve stopped really noticing. Today, do one thing that treats them as if it were the first year, not the tenth.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 15:31', headline: 'Maimonides: becoming impure is no sin; it only bars the holy.', text: 'The laws of bodily flows end with their purpose: keep Israel apart from their impurity “so they will not die in their impurity when they defile My Mishkan that is in their midst.” The danger is to the sanctuary, not the body. Maimonides is plain: the Torah’s laws of purity apply only to the Temple and its holy food. Eating impure ordinary food is no prohibition at all, and except for priests and nazirites near the dead, anyone may become impure. Birth, sex, illness and death make a person impure, and none of them is wrong.', try: 'Name one thing about your body or your health that you treat as shameful. Today, say plainly to yourself, or to someone you trust: this is not a sin.' },
    ],
  },

  'Achrei Mot': {
    meaning: 'After the death',
    summary: 'After the death of Aaron’s two sons, God tells Aaron when he may enter the Holy of Holies: only on Yom Kippur. The portion describes the Yom Kippur service, including the two goats, one offered to God and one sent into the wilderness “to Azazel,” carrying the people’s sins. It then forbids offering sacrifices outside the sanctuary and eating blood, and lists forbidden relationships.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Two goats', text: 'Two identical goats; a lottery decides which is offered to God and which is sent away to Azazel. Same start, different ends. What we choose, and what happens to us, shapes what we become.', source: 'Leviticus 16:7–10' },
      { title: 'People first', text: 'Yom Kippur brings atonement “before the LORD.” The Mishnah adds: wrongs against other people aren’t forgiven on Yom Kippur until you make peace with them yourself.', source: 'Leviticus 16:30 · Mishnah Yoma 8:9' },
      { title: 'Live by them', text: '“Keep My laws, which a person shall do and live by them.” The Talmud learns: live by them, not die by them. Saving a life comes before almost every other commandment.', source: 'Leviticus 18:5 · Yoma 85b' },
      { title: 'Approach with care', text: 'The portion opens “after the death of Aaron’s two sons, when they came too close before the LORD.” Holiness can be dangerous when it’s approached carelessly. Closeness takes care.', source: 'Leviticus 16:1–2' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Leviticus 16:16', headline: 'God dwells with Israel even inside their impurity.', text: 'On Yom Kippur the High Priest cleanses the sanctuary “of the impurities of the children of Israel,” the Tent “that dwells with them in the midst of their impurity.” The Talmud (Yoma 56b–57a) tells of a Sadducee who taunted Rabbi Chanina: you are impure, as Lamentations says, “her impurity is in her skirts,” and God’s Presence does not rest on the impure. Rabbi Chanina answered with this verse: “dwells with them in the midst of their impurity.” Even when they are impure, the Presence is among them. The whole cleansing ritual assumes that God has stayed.', try: 'Think of the stretch of your life you consider your worst. Today, name one sign that you weren’t abandoned in it: a person, a moment, a way out.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 16:21', headline: 'For forty years the scarlet thread stopped turning white.', text: 'The High Priest lays both hands on the goat’s head, confesses all of Israel’s sins over it, and a man leads it into the wilderness. Rabbi Yishmael says in the Mishnah (Yoma 6:8) that a strip of crimson wool was tied at the sanctuary door, and when the goat reached the desert it turned white: “Though your sins be like scarlet, they shall be white as snow.” The Talmud (Yoma 39b) records that in the forty years before the Temple fell, the crimson wool no longer turned white. And still, every year, they confessed and sent the goat.', try: 'Keep working today at one repair, a relationship, a promise, a habit, even though you’ve seen no sign yet that it’s working.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 16:30', headline: 'Yom Kippur cannot forgive what you did to another person.', text: '“On this day He will atone for you, to purify you; from all your sins before the LORD you shall be pure.” Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah reads the verse with a twist (Mishnah Yoma 8:9): it is the sins “before the LORD,” between you and God, that the day atones. For what you did to another person, Yom Kippur does nothing until you have made peace with them. The same Mishnah warns that “I’ll sin, and Yom Kippur will atone” does not work. The holiest day of the year sends you first to your neighbor’s door.', try: 'Who do you owe an apology that you’ve been leaving to God, or to time? Make it today, in person or by phone, not by text.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 17:4', headline: 'In the desert, killing an ox for dinner counted as shedding blood.', text: 'Whoever slaughters an ox, a lamb or a goat anywhere but at the Tent’s entrance, the Torah says, “blood shall be counted to that man: he has shed blood.” Rashi takes it at full strength: as though he had shed the blood of a human being. In the wilderness, by this law, every meal of meat had to be an offering. Later, Deuteronomy permits meat simply because you crave it, once the people spread out across the land. But this verse never stops asking its question: a life was taken for your plate, and that is not nothing.', try: 'At your next meal with meat, stop before you eat and say a blessing, or simply name the animal to yourself. Eat it knowing what it cost.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 18:5', headline: 'Shmuel: “live by them” means you are not to die by them.', text: '“Keep My laws and My rules, which a person shall do and live by them.” In the Talmud (Yoma 85b) the sages hunt for the source that saving a life overrides Shabbat. Proof after proof is offered and knocked down. Shmuel’s survives: “live by them,” and not die by them. Ravina’s verdict: one sharp pepper is better than a basket of squash. Rabbi Meir notices another word (Sanhedrin 59a): the verse doesn’t say priests, Levites or Israelites, but “a person,” so even a non-Jew who studies Torah is like the High Priest.', try: 'Is there a rule you keep, religious, professional or personal, that is quietly costing your health or someone else’s? Bend it today, for the sake of life.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 18:18', headline: 'The Torah forbids marrying two sisters, exactly what Jacob did.', text: '“Do not take a woman together with her sister, to make them rivals, while she is alive.” Rashi explains <i>litzror</i>: to make each a <i>tzarah</i>, a rival wife. Anyone who knows Genesis hears Rachel and Leah, the loved sister and the unloved one, naming their children after their rivalry. The Ramban faces it head-on: the patriarchs kept the Torah’s laws in the Land, and Jacob married the sisters outside it. Then he adds something startling. Rachel died on the road just as they entered the Land, because Jacob could not live there with two sisters.', try: 'Is there a rivalry you are feeding, between siblings, friends or colleagues, even by accident? Do one thing today that stops setting them against each other.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 18:28', headline: 'The land that spat out the Canaanites will spit out Israel too.', text: 'The chapter of forbidden relations ends with a warning that cuts both ways. The nations before you did all these things, “and the land vomited out its inhabitants.” Do the same, and “the land will vomit you out as it vomited out the nation before you.” Israel holds the land on a moral condition, not as a birthright that no behavior can lose. Rashi gives the Sifra’s image: like a prince fed something disgusting that his stomach cannot hold, the Land of Israel cannot keep sinners on it. The promise and the threat are one sentence.', try: 'Name one thing your community, family or workplace does that you know is wrong. Do one thing today to change it, even a small one.' },
    ],
  },

  'Kedoshim': {
    meaning: 'Holy',
    summary: '“You shall be holy, for I the LORD your God am holy.” This portion spells out what holiness means in daily life: respecting parents, keeping Shabbat, leaving the corners of your field for the poor, not stealing or lying, paying workers on time, not cursing the deaf or tripping the blind, judging fairly, not gossiping or taking revenge, and the famous command, “Love your neighbor as yourself.”',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Holiness is everyday', text: '“Be holy” isn’t followed by mystical secrets but by practical laws: honest scales, fair wages, kindness to the poor. Holiness is how you treat people on an ordinary Tuesday.', source: 'Leviticus 19:2–18' },
      { title: 'Love your neighbor as yourself', text: 'Rabbi Akiva called it a great principle of the Torah. Hillel said it the other way around: “What is hateful to you, don’t do to your fellow. That is the whole Torah; the rest is commentary. Go and learn it.”', source: 'Leviticus 19:18 · Shabbat 31a' },
      { title: 'Leave the corners', text: 'When you harvest, leave the corners of your field and the fallen grapes for the poor and the stranger. Some of what you earn was never meant to be all yours.', source: 'Leviticus 19:9–10' },
      { title: 'Speak up instead of stewing', text: '“Do not hate your brother in your heart; you shall surely rebuke your neighbor.” Better to speak honestly and kindly than to keep resentment inside.', source: 'Leviticus 19:17' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Leviticus 19:2', headline: 'The Ramban: you can be a scoundrel without breaking a single law.', text: '“Be holy, for I, the LORD your God, am holy.” What does holiness add to all the laws? The Ramban answers with one of his most famous lines. The Torah forbids certain foods and relations, but permits wine, meat and marriage. A man of appetite could treat that as license: drink with drunkards, gorge on meat, chase his desires within marriage, talk crudely, since none of it is expressly forbidden. He would be <i>naval birshut haTorah</i>, a scoundrel with the Torah’s permission. So the Torah adds a command over everything: be holy, and hold back even in what is allowed.', try: 'Pick one permitted thing you overdo, a drink, a meal, a screen, a way of talking. Today, take less of it on purpose, though nothing forbids it.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 19:18', headline: 'Ben Azzai found a principle greater than loving your neighbor.', text: '“Love your neighbor as yourself,” Rabbi Akiva says, is the great principle of the Torah. Ben Azzai, in the Sifra, names an even greater one: “This is the book of the generations of Adam,” the verse about God’s likeness. Bereshit Rabbah (24:7) shows the stakes: don’t say, since I was humiliated, let my friend be humiliated with me. Rabbi Tanchuma adds: if you do, know whom you are humiliating, one made “in the likeness of God.” “As yourself” fails if you think little of yourself. Your own wounds are not the measure of what others deserve.', try: 'Notice today if you’re about to pass on how you were treated: a snapped answer, a put-down, a cold shoulder. Let it stop with you.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 19:32', headline: 'Rabbi Yochanan stood up for old men who didn’t share his faith.', text: '“Rise before the gray head, and honor the face of the elder.” Rashi reads “elder” as one who has acquired wisdom, a scholar. Isi ben Yehudah reads “gray head” literally: any old person, learned or not. Rabbi Yochanan rules like Isi, and the Talmud (Kiddushin 33a) shows what that meant to him. He would stand up for elderly Arameans, non-Jews, and explain: “How many experiences have passed over them.” Not their learning, not their piety, only the years they had survived. A long life, whatever it held, was enough to bring a great sage to his feet.', try: 'Spend real time today with someone much older than you, not a relative if you can. Ask what they’ve lived through, and listen to the whole answer.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 19:36', headline: 'The God who knew Egypt’s firstborn knows your weights are rigged.', text: '“You shall have honest scales, honest weights… I am the LORD your God who brought you out of Egypt.” Why bring up the Exodus over a grocer’s scale? Rava answers in the Talmud (Bava Metzia 61b). In Egypt, God told apart which child was a firstborn and which was not, something no human could know. That same God will punish the merchant who secretly hides his weights in salt to cheat customers who can’t tell. The plague of the firstborn becomes a warning about fraud that no inspector will ever catch. Honesty is measured where only God is looking.', try: 'Check one place where you could cheat and no one would ever know: an expense, a timesheet, a tax line, a resale. Make it exactly honest today.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 20:5', headline: 'A family that shields one crook is a family of crooks.', text: 'A man gives his child to Molech, and the people of the land “hide their eyes” and do nothing. God says: “I will set My face against that man and against his family.” Rabbi Shimon asks the obvious question, what did his family do wrong, and answers it himself (Shevuot 39a): in a family with one tax collector, the corrupt kind, they are all tax collectors, because they all cover for him. Only the man himself is cut off, Rashi adds, but the family suffers for the shield they put around him. Looking away is itself a deed.', try: 'Is there someone close to you whose wrongdoing you keep explaining away to others? Today, stop defending it, and tell them plainly what you see.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 20:17', headline: 'The Torah’s word for this incest is the word for kindness.', text: 'The Torah condemns a man who takes his sister with a startling word: <i>chesed hu</i>. Usually <i>chesed</i> means loving-kindness. Rashi gives the plain sense first: in Aramaic the word means disgrace. Then the midrash (Sanhedrin 58b): if you ask, didn’t Cain marry his sister? That was a <i>chesed</i>, a kindness God did so the world could be built through him, as the psalm says, “the world is built on kindness.” What made humanity possible at the beginning is a disgrace now. The same act can be grace in one age and a crime in another.', try: 'Think of a habit or rule that once saved you, in a hard year or an old job, and now does harm. Let go of it today, with thanks for what it did.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 20:26', headline: 'Don’t say you hate pork; say you want it and won’t.', text: '“I have set you apart from the peoples to be Mine.” Rashi quotes Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah: don’t say, “Pork disgusts me,” or “I can’t stand wearing wool and linen.” Say, “I would like to, but what can I do? My Father in heaven has forbidden it.” Maimonides, in his Eight Chapters, sets this against the philosophers, who prize the person with no bad desires at all. Both are right, he says. For murder, theft and lies, better not to want them. For the Torah’s decrees, wanting and refraining is the higher thing.', try: 'Keep one commitment today that you’d honestly rather skip, and admit to yourself exactly what you’d rather be doing. Then do it anyway.' },
    ],
  },

  'Emor': {
    meaning: 'Speak',
    summary: 'Special rules for the priests: whom they may marry, avoiding contact with the dead except their closest family, and why offerings must be without blemish. Then the Torah lists the holy days of the year: Shabbat, Passover and the counting of the Omer, Shavuot, Rosh Hashanah, Yom Kippur and Sukkot. It ends with the oil for the menorah, the bread on the golden table, and a man who curses God’s name in a fight.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Appointments with God', text: 'The festivals are called <i>mo’adim</i>, “meeting times”: appointments with God built into the calendar. A good life needs fixed times for joy, reflection and rest.', source: 'Leviticus 23:2' },
      { title: 'Count the days', text: 'From Passover to Shavuot we count forty-nine days, the Omer. Freedom (Passover) has to grow into purpose (Shavuot, the giving of the Torah). Counting makes each day matter.', source: 'Leviticus 23:15–16' },
      { title: 'Joy is a commandment', text: 'Sukkot is the festival of joy: “You shall rejoice before the LORD your God seven days.” Celebrating is a commandment too.', source: 'Leviticus 23:40' },
      { title: 'Words in anger', text: 'The portion ends with a man who curses God’s name during a fight. Our words, especially in anger, have power over people and over holiness itself.', source: 'Leviticus 24:10–16' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Leviticus 21:1', headline: 'The High Priest can’t bury his father, but must bury a stranger.', text: '“None shall become impure for the dead among his people,” God tells the priests, except for close family. The High Priest may not become impure even for his own father or mother. But Rashi reads a limit into “among his people”: only while the dead is among people who can bury him. The <i>met mitzvah</i>, a body with no one to tend it, is different. The Mishnah (Nazir 7:1) rules that the High Priest himself must become impure and bury that stranger. Holiness that keeps him from his father’s grave cannot keep him from the one nobody else will touch.', try: 'Find the task near you that nobody is doing because it belongs to no one: an unvisited neighbor, a neglected chore, a forgotten grave. Do it today.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 21:22', headline: 'Maimonides admits why a blemished priest couldn’t serve: crowds judge by looks.', text: 'A priest who is blind, lame, disfigured or hunchbacked may not offer the bread of his God. Maimonides doesn’t pretend this is about the man’s worth. In the <i>Guide</i> he is plain: the multitude does not judge a person by his true form but by the perfection of his limbs, and the Temple had to be held in awe. The rule bows to the crowd’s prejudice. But the Torah adds, in the next breath: “the bread of his God, from the holiest and the holy, he shall eat.” Kept from the altar, he is never put out of the family.', try: 'Notice one person today whom others judge by appearance: an accent, a disability, an age, a face. Make a point of treating them by their true form.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 22:27', headline: 'God takes offerings only from the hunted, never from the hunters.', text: '“An ox, a sheep or a goat, when it is born…” Why these three? The midrash (Vayikra Rabbah 27:5) answers with Ecclesiastes: “God seeks the pursued.” The ox is hunted by the lion, the goat by the leopard, the sheep by the wolf. God says: do not bring Me the pursuers, bring Me the pursued. The midrash runs the rule through history: Abel chosen over Cain, Noah over his generation, Israel over the nations. And it goes further than comfort allows: even when a righteous person is pursuing a wicked one, God is on the side of the pursued.', try: 'In a conflict around you this week, at work, online, in the family, notice who is being hunted. Stand beside them, even if you think they’re partly wrong.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 23:4', headline: 'Rabbi Akiva: the festivals fall when people declare them, even wrongly.', text: '“These are the LORD’s appointed times, which you shall proclaim in their season.” The calendar is handed to people. The Talmud (Rosh Hashanah 25a) tests how far that goes. Rabban Gamliel fixes the new moon on testimony Rabbi Yehoshua thinks is false, and orders him to come with his staff and money on the day Yehoshua believes is Yom Kippur. Rabbi Akiva consoles him with this verse: declared at the right time or not, these are My festivals. Yehoshua comes. Rabban Gamliel stands, kisses his head, and says: “Come in peace, my teacher and my student.”', try: 'Is there a decision you lost, at work or in the family, that you keep fighting quietly? Accept it out loud today, and help make it work.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 23:32', headline: 'Eating the day before Yom Kippur counts as fasting.', text: '“You shall afflict yourselves on the ninth of the month, in the evening.” But the fast is on the tenth. Chiyya bar Rav of Difti explains (Yoma 81b): the Torah mentions the ninth to teach that whoever eats and drinks on the ninth and fasts on the tenth is credited as if he had fasted both days. The feast before the fast is part of the fast. Judaism doesn’t ask you to walk into the holiest day already starving; it asks you to arrive strong, fed and ready. Preparing the body is itself a religious act.', try: 'Before the hardest thing on your schedule this week, plan how you’ll prepare your body for it: sleep, a real meal, a walk. Do the first of those today.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 23:43', headline: 'At harvest, with your barns full, you move into a hut.', text: '“So that your generations will know that I had the Israelites live in huts when I brought them out of Egypt.” Why in autumn? The Rashbam answers with the calendar: you build the sukkah at the harvest, when your houses are full of grain, wine and oil. Right then you leave the full house and sit in a hut, remembering forty years in a desert where Israel owned no land. Then you will be grateful to the One who gave you all this. The moment you’re likeliest to feel self-made is the moment the Torah sends you outside.', try: 'In the thing you’re proudest of earning, name three people or lucky breaks that made it possible. Thank one of them today.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 24:10', headline: 'The blasphemer cursed God after the camp told him he didn’t belong.', text: '“The son of an Israelite woman and an Egyptian man went out,” fights in the camp and curses the Name. From where? Rashi brings the midrash: he tried to pitch his tent with his mother’s tribe, Dan. They said, “What are you doing here?” Each man camps by his father’s house, and his father was Egyptian. He took it to Moses’ court, lost, and came out cursing God. The Torah punishes him and does not excuse him. But it keeps the story of a man with no place in the camp, and a rule that left him nowhere to stand.', try: 'Think of someone in your circle who doesn’t quite belong, by background, family or history. Make sure today that they have a place to pitch their tent.' },
    ],
  },

  'Behar': {
    meaning: 'On the mountain',
    summary: 'At Mount Sinai, God gives the laws of the land: every seventh year is a Shabbat for the land, the <i>shmita</i>, when fields rest. Every fiftieth year is the Jubilee, when land goes back to its original families and servants go free. The portion forbids cheating in business and charging interest to someone in need, and commands helping a struggling neighbor before he falls.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'The land needs rest too', text: 'Every seventh year, the fields rest. The earth isn’t just a resource. It belongs to God, and it needs a Shabbat too.', source: 'Leviticus 25:2–5' },
      { title: 'Proclaim liberty', text: 'In the Jubilee year, “proclaim liberty throughout the land to all its inhabitants.” The words are engraved on the Liberty Bell in Philadelphia.', source: 'Leviticus 25:10' },
      { title: 'Catch people before they fall', text: '“If your brother becomes poor… hold him up.” Rashi: don’t let him fall all the way. A load is easy to steady while it’s still on the donkey. Maimonides calls helping someone stand on their own the highest kind of charity.', source: 'Leviticus 25:35 · Rashi · Rambam, Gifts to the Poor 10:7' },
      { title: 'Honest business', text: '“When you sell to your neighbor or buy from your neighbor, do not wrong one another.” Prices should be fair; the Talmud even set limits on overcharging.', source: 'Leviticus 25:14 · Bava Metzia 49b' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Leviticus 25:10', headline: 'Liberty could be proclaimed only while every tribe was home.', text: '“Proclaim liberty throughout the land to all its inhabitants.” On Yom Kippur of the fiftieth year the shofar sounds, servants go free and land returns to its families. Rashi explains <i>dror</i>, liberty: a free man is like a guest who can lodge wherever he pleases. The Talmud (Arachin 32b) reads “all its inhabitants” strictly. Once the tribes of Reuben, Gad and half of Manasseh were exiled, the Jubilee stopped, even with most of the people still in the land. Liberty in the Torah is not for a majority. It can be proclaimed only when everyone is home.', try: 'Think of someone missing from a table you sit at: family, friends, a community. Reach out today and make it easier for them to come back.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 25:17', headline: 'Cruel words are worse than cheating, because money can be returned.', text: 'Twice the Torah says “do not wrong one another.” The first time is about prices; the second adds “and fear your God,” and the Talmud (Bava Metzia 58b) says it means wronging with words. Don’t remind a penitent of what he used to do. Don’t ask a shopkeeper the price when you have no plan to buy. Rabbi Yochanan, citing Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai: verbal wrong is worse than cheating, since only it carries “fear your God.” Rabbi Elazar: one hurts the body, the other money. Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani: money can be returned; words cannot.', try: 'Think of a cutting thing you said this month that you can’t take back. Go to that person today and say the true, kind thing you should have said instead.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 25:21', headline: 'Trust gets a quiet blessing; worry gets one you can see.', text: 'Keep the seventh year, God promises, and “you will eat your fill.” Rashi and the Sforno read that as a blessing inside the food itself: a little will satisfy, the way one measure of manna fed a child and a grown man alike. Then the Torah hears the question: “What will we eat in the seventh year?” And it answers: “I will command My blessing in the sixth year,” a harvest big enough for three years. The Sforno says this second blessing is made to satisfy the eyes of the doubters. God answers anxiety too, only with a different gift.', try: 'Notice something you’re anxiously stockpiling: money, reassurance, backup plans. Today, trust a little, and let one small surplus go to someone who needs it.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 25:25', headline: 'You may sell your family’s land only when poverty forces you.', text: '“If your brother becomes poor and sells part of his holding, his nearest redeemer shall come and buy back what his brother sold.” Rashi reads two rules into the opening words: a person may sell his inherited land only under the pressure of poverty, and never all of it; he should always keep some ground of his own. Then the redeemer, the <i>go’el</i>, is meant to step in. The Book of Ruth shows how that can fail. The nearest relative refuses, “lest I ruin my own inheritance,” and the story doesn’t even record his name.', try: 'Is there a family duty you’ve dodged because it would cost you, the way Ruth’s redeemer feared for his own estate? Do one concrete piece of it today.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 25:35', headline: 'One person can steady a slipping load; five can’t lift it fallen.', text: '“If your brother becomes poor and his hand falters beside you, hold him up.” Rashi takes “hold him up” literally: don’t wait until he has fallen all the way. A load slipping on a donkey’s back can be steadied by one person; once it hits the ground, five can’t lift it. Maimonides builds the highest of his eight levels of charity on this verse: a gift, a loan, a partnership or a job that strengthens a person before he falls, so he never has to ask. The best help often looks as if nothing happened at all.', try: 'Think of someone whose hand is starting to falter, in work, money or health. Offer specific help today, before they have to ask for it.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 25:46', headline: 'The law allows a harsh master; Maimonides says piety demands mercy.', text: 'The chapter that frees Hebrew servants permits something hard: slaves bought from the nations “you may keep as slaves forever; but over your brothers, the Israelites, do not rule harshly.” The Torah is honest that its world had slavery. Maimonides rules that the law allows a non-Hebrew slave to be worked hard, and then turns: the way of piety and wisdom is mercy. Feed them from every dish you eat. Don’t shame them, don’t shout at them, listen to their claims. And he quotes Job: “Did not He who made me in the womb make him?”', try: 'Think of someone who works for you or serves you: a cleaner, a cashier, a junior colleague. Today, listen fully to one of their complaints, and act on it.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 25:55', headline: 'The ear that heard Sinai is pierced for choosing a human master.', text: '“For the Israelites are My servants, whom I brought out of Egypt.” Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai uses this verse to explain one of the strangest rites in the Torah (Kiddushin 22b): a servant who refuses to go free has his ear pierced at the doorpost. Why the ear? It heard at Sinai, “they are My servants,” not servants of servants, and still went and acquired a master. And the doorpost, adds Rabbi Shimon, witnessed the Exodus. The Talmud (Bava Metzia 10a) draws a law from the same verse: a hired worker may quit even in the middle of the day.', try: 'Name one thing you serve that you never really chose: a habit, a job’s grip on your evenings, someone’s approval. Take back one hour from it today.' },
    ],
  },

  'Bechukotai': {
    meaning: 'In My laws',
    summary: 'God promises blessings if the people follow the commandments: rain in its season, rich harvests, peace, and God’s presence among them. Then come warnings of what will happen if they don’t: disease, defeat and exile. Even then, God promises never to abandon them, and to remember the covenant. The portion, and the book of Leviticus, ends with laws about vows and gifts to the sanctuary.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Keep walking', text: '“If you walk in My laws…” Rashi says walking means working hard at learning. Growth is a path you keep walking, not a place you arrive.', source: 'Leviticus 26:3 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Never abandoned', text: 'After the hardest warnings in the Torah, God promises: “Even then, I will not reject them or cast them away… I will remember My covenant.” No matter how far people fall, the relationship isn’t over.', source: 'Leviticus 26:44–45' },
      { title: 'Peace is the blessing', text: 'Of all the blessings, Rashi singles out “I will give peace in the land”: without peace, the rest is worth nothing.', source: 'Leviticus 26:6 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Everyone has a value', text: 'The last chapter sets a value for each person pledged to the sanctuary, from babies to the elderly. Every person, of every age, counts.', source: 'Leviticus 27:1–8' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Leviticus 26:3', headline: 'The blessings depend on toiling in Torah, not on mastering it.', text: '“If you walk in My statutes and keep My commandments and do them, I will give your rains in their season.” If keeping and doing the commandments are already listed, Rashi asks, what is “walking in My statutes”? His answer, from the Sifra: that you labor in the Torah, <i>amelim baTorah</i>. Not that you know it all, finish it or always get it right, but that you toil at it, intending to keep what you learn. The whole chain of blessings, rain, harvest, peace, hangs first on effort. The Torah rewards the struggle before the result.', try: 'Pick one hard thing you’ve been avoiding learning: a text, a skill, a language. Give it thirty minutes of real effort today, without judging how far you get.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 26:6', headline: 'King Josiah died trusting a blessing his generation hadn’t earned.', text: '“I will give peace in the land… and no sword shall pass through your land.” Rashi: not even an army marching through on its way to another war. The Talmud (Taanit 22b) tells what that verse cost. When Pharaoh Necho sought to pass through to fight elsewhere, the righteous King Josiah went out to stop him, relying on this verse. He did not ask the prophet Jeremiah, and did not know his generation did not merit the blessing. Egyptian arrows made his body like a sieve. Dying, he said: “The LORD is righteous, for I rebelled against His word.”', try: 'Is there a decision you’re about to make on the strength of a principle alone? Before you act, ask one wise person who knows the situation.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 26:21', headline: 'Maimonides: calling your troubles mere chance is a kind of cruelty.', text: 'The curses of this chapter turn on one strange word: “If you walk with Me <i>keri</i>.” Rashi brings the sages’ reading: by chance, casually, now and then. And God answers in kind: then I will walk with you in the fury of <i>keri</i>. Maimonides turns it into law. When trouble comes and people say, “It’s just nature, it’s just what happens,” that is a cruel way of seeing things, because it keeps them attached to the very deeds that need to change. The answer to hardship is to cry out and examine yourself, not to shrug.', try: 'Take one recurring problem in your life that you’ve been calling bad luck. Today, write down honestly what part of it might be yours to change.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 27:3', headline: 'The Torah prices the most beautiful and the plainest person the same.', text: 'Vow your “value” to the sanctuary and you pay a fixed sum: fifty shekels for a man of twenty to sixty, thirty for a woman, less for the young and old. The Mishnah (Arachin 3:1) insists the table ignores everything else: the most beautiful person in Israel and the most unsightly are valued the same. The table, valuing a woman below a man, is hard to read today. The Talmud (Arachin 19a) notices that after sixty her value falls less than his, and quotes a saying: an old man in the house is a burden; an old woman, a treasure.', try: 'Notice today when you size someone up by looks, title or money. Correct it on the spot: give them the attention you’d give the most impressive person in the room.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 27:18', headline: 'Before God, a field’s worth is the years left, not its soil.', text: 'Dedicate an inherited field to the sanctuary, and its redemption price is set “by its seed”: fifty shekels for land that takes a homer of barley to sow. Rashi stresses that a good field and a poor one cost the same, and the Mishnah (Arachin 3:2) names the extremes, sandy plots and the orchards of Sebastia. What changes the price is time. Each year closer to the Jubilee, Rashi says, the price drops, because the field returns to its family then. Every holding is measured against the Jubilee. What you really hold is years.', try: 'Count the years you likely have left with something you treat as permanent: a parent, a home, your health. Spend one real hour of today on it.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 27:28', headline: 'Even to God, you may not give away everything you own.', text: '“Anything a man devotes to the LORD, from all that he has…” The rabbis read “from” closely: some, not all. Rabbi Eliezer rules in the Mishnah (Arachin 8:4) that a person may devote some of his flock, some of his servants, some of his field, but if he devotes everything, it does not take effect. Rabbi Elazar ben Azaryah draws the conclusion: if even for the Most High you may not give all your property away, how much more should you not give it all to others. Maimonides is blunt: that is not piety but foolishness.', try: 'If you’re a giver who empties yourself, of money, time or energy, set one clear limit today and keep it, without apologizing.' },
      { verse: 'Leviticus 27:34', headline: 'Leviticus ends by closing heaven’s door on new laws.', text: '“These are the commandments the LORD commanded Moses for the Israelites at Mount Sinai.” The Talmud (Temurah 16a) reads “these” as a seal: from now on, no prophet may add a law. It tells how three thousand laws were forgotten in the mourning for Moses. The people begged Joshua to ask heaven; he answered, “It is not in heaven.” Generations later they asked Samuel, and he quoted this verse. Rabbi Abbahu adds that Othniel ben Kenaz restored lost teachings by sharp reasoning. From here on, Torah is rebuilt by minds arguing, not by voices from the sky.', try: 'Is there a question you keep waiting for a sign about? Sit down today, reason it through on paper, and decide.' },
    ],
  },
});
